1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:14,001
It has become a new dimension in entertainment.

2
00:00:14,860 --> 00:00:16,820
It pulls us into the center of the action.

3
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:20,800
With explosive sounds and computer-controlled audio design,

4
00:00:21,140 --> 00:00:23,640
from its humble beginnings in the basements of amateurs,

5
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:27,440
stereo has grown into a multi-billion dollar industry.

6
00:00:27,820 --> 00:00:29,380
Now, stereo.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,360
and the sound revolution on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:55,660 --> 00:00:56,860
STEREO

9
00:00:56,861 --> 00:00:59,640
Today stereo draws us into the action.

10
00:00:59,641 --> 00:01:09,640
Dynamic sound sends shivers up our spine in theaters, relaxes us at home, entertains us in the car.

11
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:19,640
The story of stereo is the story of invention and transformation.

12
00:01:19,641 --> 00:01:24,640
At first, sound reproduction was manoral, or single channel.

13
00:01:24,641 --> 00:01:31,180
But since we listen with two ears, two-channel or stereo sound became the goal.

14
00:01:33,460 --> 00:01:37,080
Stereo means three-dimensional or solid in Greek.

15
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:42,100
It means that the sound field is coming from all directions around you.

16
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:48,200
The sound revolution was a quest by visionaries for the perfect listening experience.

17
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:52,540
It was a quest for the impossible, audio perfection.

18
00:01:52,541 --> 00:01:58,500
What technology is now making it possible to do is get closer and closer to capturing

19
00:01:58,501 --> 00:02:01,960
the reality of how that voice really sounds.

20
00:02:01,961 --> 00:02:07,500
In the way that I sit here and I say something, I don't really sound like this.

21
00:02:07,501 --> 00:02:12,220
You know, you're getting close to it, but it's still not a perfect reality.

22
00:02:12,221 --> 00:02:20,260
Stereo didn't debut until 1957, but it had a long history of thrilling sound breakthroughs.

23
00:02:20,261 --> 00:02:26,260
As early as the 1920s, America was tapping its feet to amazing innovations that brought

24
00:02:26,261 --> 00:02:28,260
music into the home.

25
00:02:28,261 --> 00:02:32,260
The revolution in audio had begun.

26
00:02:32,261 --> 00:02:45,500
At the turn of the century, a spirit of invention was sweeping America.

27
00:02:45,501 --> 00:02:50,580
And one of the things inventors pursued was a better understanding of the physical characteristics

28
00:02:50,581 --> 00:02:53,300
of sound.

29
00:02:53,301 --> 00:02:56,400
Sound is basically just vibrations in the air.

30
00:02:56,401 --> 00:03:03,240
A vibrating object such as a string of a musical instrument makes the air vibrate, the vibrations

31
00:03:03,241 --> 00:03:09,280
travel through the air, they go into our ears, they make our eardrums vibrate, and our brains

32
00:03:09,281 --> 00:03:12,480
read the vibrations and interpret them as sound.

33
00:03:12,481 --> 00:03:19,240
Thomas Edison and Alexander Graham Bell were professional rivals and fierce competitors.

34
00:03:19,241 --> 00:03:23,720
Their two inventions, the telephone and the phonograph, provided the fundamental building

35
00:03:23,721 --> 00:03:30,360
blocks for stereo. Edison's phonograph was the first to actually capture sound.

36
00:03:30,361 --> 00:03:35,460
The way all phonograph recording works, whether you're working on a cylinder or a disc, is

37
00:03:35,461 --> 00:03:40,560
you have a stylus called a cutting stylus, and something makes it move in response to

38
00:03:40,561 --> 00:03:47,840
the signal and as it moves a recording medium a disc or cylinder is being rotated past it

39
00:03:47,841 --> 00:03:51,773
and those motions are recorded in the groove.

40
00:03:51,774 --> 00:03:55,880
It was Edison's voice that made the stylus move

41
00:03:55,881 --> 00:03:59,659
in his famous first recording of a children's nursery rhyme.

42
00:03:59,660 --> 00:04:01,360
Mary had a little lamb, its

43
00:04:01,361 --> 00:04:06,684
fleas was white as snow and everywhere that Mary went the lamb was sure to go.

44
00:04:06,685 --> 00:04:07,640
Original sound

45
00:04:07,641 --> 00:04:09,250
recording was monaural.

46
00:04:09,251 --> 00:04:13,520
Thomas Alva Edison recorded a cylinder and it was from a horn

47
00:04:13,521 --> 00:04:19,098
to the cylinder itself and out back from the horn to listeners.

48
00:04:19,099 --> 00:04:21,400
It did not invoke anything

49
00:04:21,401 --> 00:04:24,249
about differences between the two ears.

50
00:04:24,250 --> 00:04:28,120
Edison's phonograph captured the natural power of the

51
00:04:28,121 --> 00:04:34,020
human voice. Alexander Graham Bell's telephone electrified the human voice.

52
00:04:34,021 --> 00:04:38,260
Telephone, like any new invention, was a marvel. It worked.

53
00:04:38,700 --> 00:04:41,040
But then they started discovering problems with it.

54
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:47,560
One problem was that the farther away you got from the person who was speaking,

55
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:49,820
the weaker the signal got.

56
00:04:52,140 --> 00:04:56,560
Another was that the sound, even when you were close, wasn't necessarily always all that clear.

57
00:04:57,460 --> 00:05:00,820
The vacuum tube, when combined with a power source,

58
00:05:00,821 --> 00:05:07,780
provided a way to translate the acoustic audio signal into a more powerful electrical impulse.

59
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:16,180
In order to perfect the telephone network, they needed to be able to record the sound.

60
00:05:18,020 --> 00:05:23,380
The phonograph of the day was an acoustic mechanical device. There's no electricity

61
00:05:23,381 --> 00:05:24,271
involved.

62
00:05:24,272 --> 00:05:32,880
This led a team of researchers at AT&T, led by Joseph Maxfield, to devise an electrical

63
00:05:32,881 --> 00:05:39,680
phonograph, originally for the purpose of doing the research necessary to make a better telephone

64
00:05:39,681 --> 00:05:40,381
signal.

65
00:05:40,382 --> 00:05:46,720
By 1926, thanks to Maxfield, the phonograph and the telephone had caught up to one another.

66
00:05:46,721 --> 00:05:53,300
the amplified phonograph quickly found its way out of the lab and into the living room.

67
00:05:54,180 --> 00:05:56,860
America loved listening to its favorite music.

68
00:06:02,040 --> 00:06:04,440
Stereo was still a few years away,

69
00:06:04,780 --> 00:06:10,580
but audio professionals couldn't wait to put newly invented audio technology to use.

70
00:06:10,581 --> 00:06:18,920
AT&T did its first large-scale tests of public address systems in 1916.

71
00:06:21,260 --> 00:06:25,500
By the early 20s, these were a standard item of commerce.

72
00:06:26,260 --> 00:06:31,020
Warren Harding's inaugural address as President of the United States in 1921

73
00:06:31,021 --> 00:06:37,940
was heard by an audience of 150,000 on the mall in Washington

74
00:06:37,941 --> 00:06:42,460
over an AT&T Western Electric public address system.

75
00:06:44,060 --> 00:06:46,860
Loudspeakers brought big sound outdoors,

76
00:06:47,280 --> 00:06:51,160
but one audio pioneer was about to move sound indoors.

77
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:56,180
The first person to actually get sound on film was Eugene Laust,

78
00:06:56,440 --> 00:06:59,440
who had worked for Edison, moved to England,

79
00:06:59,441 --> 00:07:03,400
and in 1908 actually managed to get sound on and off film.

80
00:07:03,960 --> 00:07:06,240
We have a photograph of him recording in his back garden

81
00:07:06,241 --> 00:07:07,780
with a lady in a crinoline dress,

82
00:07:07,940 --> 00:07:10,220
and a boom mic and a big camera.

83
00:07:10,820 --> 00:07:12,780
And he shot under 35mm film,

84
00:07:13,340 --> 00:07:16,200
and literally half the frame, which is about one inch across,

85
00:07:16,720 --> 00:07:18,780
half the frame was used for picture and half for sound.

86
00:07:19,620 --> 00:07:24,100
Laust's ingenuity was to literally photograph the human voice,

87
00:07:24,760 --> 00:07:26,840
transforming it into a pattern of light

88
00:07:26,841 --> 00:07:30,540
that could be filmed and later decoded and played back.

89
00:07:31,520 --> 00:07:33,240
Now, why in the year 1910

90
00:07:33,241 --> 00:07:35,780
didn't everybody suddenly start recording sound with picture?

91
00:07:35,781 --> 00:07:40,880
And the answer was, of course, that it was extremely complicated to do, very expensive to do,

92
00:07:41,280 --> 00:07:42,641
and nobody's particularly interested.

93
00:07:42,642 --> 00:07:44,700
They're quite happy with subtitles, thank you very much.

94
00:07:45,660 --> 00:07:48,700
Sound had come a long way since Edison's day.

95
00:07:49,340 --> 00:07:52,360
The next big step was to record in stereo.

96
00:07:54,240 --> 00:07:57,780
A.C. Keller was a brilliant young scientist at Bell Labs,

97
00:07:57,960 --> 00:08:01,500
but it was his love of music that led him to an important breakthrough.

98
00:08:01,501 --> 00:08:15,501
If he was going to produce greater quality sound recordings, which perhaps might feed back into AT&T's motion picture sound business, he wanted to get the best

99
00:08:16,956 --> 00:08:18,900
performers he could.

100
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:30,840
Keller hooked up with Leopold Stokowski and the Philadelphia Orchestra, the most famous conductors in the United States of the era, and one of the top orchestras.

101
00:08:31,500 --> 00:08:36,540
Together the scientist and the symphony conductor would change the way America

102
00:08:36,541 --> 00:08:42,180
listened to music. Stereophonic sound was really invented by Bell Labs for

103
00:08:42,181 --> 00:08:46,860
playing the Philadelphia Orchestra in a demonstration into a hall in Washington

104
00:08:46,861 --> 00:08:52,080
DC. Three microphones spaced across the orchestra in Philadelphia were picked up

105
00:08:52,081 --> 00:08:55,820
amplified sent over phone lines to Washington and played back over three

106
00:08:55,821 --> 00:09:00,820
corresponding loudspeakers. Listeners heard very much the same concert that

107
00:09:00,821 --> 00:09:03,740
They would have heard had they been in Philadelphia instead of Washington.

108
00:09:04,240 --> 00:09:08,820
So this was the first case of multi-channel stereophonic sound, 1934.

109
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,980
Creating stereo over phone lines was exciting.

110
00:09:13,820 --> 00:09:19,900
But Keller's next step, recording stereo on a disc, would change the audio world forever.

111
00:09:20,680 --> 00:09:29,400
To do this originally, what Keller did was take two recording styluses, two tone arms,

112
00:09:29,401 --> 00:09:31,680
and hooked them up together,

113
00:09:32,900 --> 00:09:36,760
producing a recording master that had two tracks on it,

114
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,200
much like two bands on a later LP,

115
00:09:41,280 --> 00:09:43,540
except that one band would be the left channel

116
00:09:43,541 --> 00:09:45,340
and one band would be the right channel.

117
00:09:45,341 --> 00:09:52,800
A little bit later, Keller figured out how to record both tracks in one groove

118
00:09:52,801 --> 00:09:57,580
by recording the two tracks at 45 degrees from each other.

119
00:09:57,581 --> 00:10:01,580
Recording two channels in the same groove was the key.

120
00:10:01,581 --> 00:10:05,580
To do this, Keller invented a machine with two styli

121
00:10:05,581 --> 00:10:08,580
that cut separate grooves on a gold disc.

122
00:10:08,581 --> 00:10:11,580
Later, a record copy was made.

123
00:10:11,581 --> 00:10:14,580
During playback, the needle would pick up both sides of the groove

124
00:10:14,581 --> 00:10:19,580
that the two styli had cut, making two channels of sound, or stereo.

125
00:10:19,581 --> 00:10:25,580
Keller's method transformed stereo from the possible to the practical.

126
00:10:25,581 --> 00:10:33,660
But a new invention, the radio was sweeping America, and it momentarily swept away any interest in Keller's work.

127
00:10:34,600 --> 00:10:37,560
Families stayed at home to hear their favorite programs.

128
00:10:38,520 --> 00:10:43,200
To lure the audience back into the theaters, the film industry turned to sound.

129
00:10:44,520 --> 00:10:48,280
1928 marked the start of the sound era in movies.

130
00:10:48,760 --> 00:10:52,340
Everyone was amazed, and Al Jolson said, you ain't heard nothing yet.

131
00:10:52,341 --> 00:10:56,020
Well, he was in mono, and only from one channel.

132
00:10:56,160 --> 00:10:57,700
Indeed, they hadn't heard anything yet.

133
00:11:01,020 --> 00:11:04,360
Fantasia was the first commercial use of stereophonic sound.

134
00:11:04,740 --> 00:11:07,920
Disney engineers took this into a whole new world,

135
00:11:08,260 --> 00:11:11,660
and what they said at the time is they needed to inveigle the public

136
00:11:11,661 --> 00:11:15,600
away from such other pastimes as baseball games and bowling alleys

137
00:11:15,601 --> 00:11:19,680
and get them back to theaters because the novelty of sound had worn off.

138
00:11:19,681 --> 00:11:24,460
To do this, they needed wider frequency range, wider dynamic range,

139
00:11:24,760 --> 00:11:28,880
and most especially stereophonic multi-channel sound with surround sound.

140
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:33,320
It was a spectacular invention for 1938-1939.

141
00:11:33,780 --> 00:11:39,700
But the explosive threat of war forced America to focus on more pressing issues

142
00:11:39,701 --> 00:11:46,320
and place the pursuit of perfect sound on hold until the enemy abroad could be vanquished.

143
00:11:49,680 --> 00:11:59,500
By the 1940s, the success of Disney's Fantasia had proven that audiences loved stereo in movies.

144
00:12:00,160 --> 00:12:05,040
But in the early 40s, the tempest of war was raging across Europe.

145
00:12:05,860 --> 00:12:10,140
And the best minds in America were working to battle an enemy overseas.

146
00:12:11,220 --> 00:12:17,760
While most research was redirected to sonar and radar, stereo was used to deceive the enemy.

147
00:12:19,680 --> 00:12:28,608
And the way they did it was to have a truck in which was sighted an optical playback dummy, a playback machine, like a projector that doesn't show picture, just plays

148
00:12:28,609 --> 00:12:29,309
back sound.

149
00:12:29,310 --> 00:12:36,866
And they would get a couple of these trucks and park them in the woods and have big cinema loudspeakers on top of the truck, crank it up, play it as loud as they could

150
00:12:36,867 --> 00:12:40,080
until the Germans and Italians believed that this was the big landing.

151
00:12:40,800 --> 00:12:44,420
The Allies weren't the only ones to use sound for deception.

152
00:12:45,220 --> 00:12:48,380
During the war, U.S. intelligence plotting attacks on Hitler

153
00:12:48,381 --> 00:12:51,540
would be certain they had located him in one city,

154
00:12:51,940 --> 00:12:55,160
only to hear him a short time later on the radio in another city.

155
00:12:57,400 --> 00:12:59,820
The riddle was solved at war's end.

156
00:13:00,740 --> 00:13:03,540
German scientists working with the wire recorder

157
00:13:03,541 --> 00:13:07,120
had laid the foundation for stereo tape recording.

158
00:13:07,960 --> 00:13:11,220
The wire recorder and tape recorder basically work the same way.

159
00:13:11,221 --> 00:13:18,520
you have a magnetizable medium, a long, long piece of it,

160
00:13:18,980 --> 00:13:22,280
that's pulled continuously past a recording head,

161
00:13:23,120 --> 00:13:27,360
and the audio signal being fed to that recording head

162
00:13:27,361 --> 00:13:30,580
creates a fluctuating magnetic field,

163
00:13:31,240 --> 00:13:37,460
which changes the magnetization of the recording medium as it goes by.

164
00:13:37,461 --> 00:13:50,704
In playback, it can go by exactly the same head, and now its magnetic field is generating a current coming out of that head, which is then amplified and becomes an

165
00:13:50,705 --> 00:13:51,760
audio signal.

166
00:13:55,940 --> 00:14:01,680
The war was over. American troops returned home ready to work and play.

167
00:14:07,460 --> 00:14:17,700
America was falling in love with the young new stars that the music industry was busy promoting.

168
00:14:18,420 --> 00:14:20,180
The crooners were still popular.

169
00:14:20,860 --> 00:14:23,960
But singers like Frank Sinatra appealed to America's youth.

170
00:14:24,800 --> 00:14:31,380
In fact, popular singers sold so many mono records that the big companies saw little need for improvement.

171
00:14:31,381 --> 00:14:35,580
But a growing number of hobbyists saw it differently.

172
00:14:35,581 --> 00:14:38,460
They wanted to hear the songs more clearly.

173
00:14:38,461 --> 00:14:42,300
There had always been record-playing devices and radios,

174
00:14:42,660 --> 00:14:45,140
or RCA and Stromberg Carlson and Magnavox,

175
00:14:45,320 --> 00:14:49,820
which made things that didn't sound as good as they could,

176
00:14:51,100 --> 00:14:54,320
and let's assume that people didn't want the good sound.

177
00:14:54,420 --> 00:14:55,720
I don't know why they didn't make sound,

178
00:14:55,820 --> 00:14:59,520
but you have the equivalent today that had the purveyors of coffee

179
00:14:59,521 --> 00:15:02,040
widely made available, really good coffee,

180
00:15:02,220 --> 00:15:03,220
there wouldn't have been a Starbucks.

181
00:15:03,221 --> 00:15:06,840
and it's an exact analogy to what happened then.

182
00:15:08,100 --> 00:15:13,840
By the early 50s, sound enthusiasts began looking for ways to improve audio equipment.

183
00:15:14,540 --> 00:15:17,760
They were able to buy surplus parts from shops on a street

184
00:15:17,761 --> 00:15:21,020
that soon became synonymous with hi-fi sound.

185
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:26,080
Cortland Street was, I like to think of it as an almost mythical place.

186
00:15:26,081 --> 00:15:33,740
It was an area in Lower Manhattan where there was a heavy concentration of electronic stores,

187
00:15:34,560 --> 00:15:40,000
and you could get almost anything in the electronic world there.

188
00:15:40,520 --> 00:15:49,440
Used, new, good, bad, and it was very common for an enthusiast,

189
00:15:49,441 --> 00:15:56,180
which is what I was at that time, to make a trip down to Courtland Street in Lower Manhattan

190
00:15:56,181 --> 00:15:59,960
and find all kinds of goodies on the shelves there.

191
00:16:00,920 --> 00:16:05,740
This was the source of parts for a lot of home constructors.

192
00:16:06,960 --> 00:16:11,360
The pursuit for better audio was part science and part passion.

193
00:16:11,361 --> 00:16:22,410
I was in college at MIT as a student and heard about the fact that you could buy wholesale components that were made for the public address business and actually make

194
00:16:22,411 --> 00:16:24,340
a good music system yourself.

195
00:16:25,060 --> 00:16:28,880
One of the things that could be found on Cortland Street was a Heath kit.

196
00:16:29,440 --> 00:16:34,160
It put high-five in the hands of anyone with a soldering iron and a little enthusiasm.

197
00:16:34,920 --> 00:16:40,000
The birth of a multi-billion dollar industry had its humble beginnings in the hobbyist's basement.

198
00:16:40,001 --> 00:16:44,200
Heathkit provided very good instructions and components.

199
00:16:45,160 --> 00:16:48,940
It was the lowest cost way to get a high-performing piece of equipment.

200
00:16:49,580 --> 00:16:54,660
While hobbyists tinkered, the rest of the country turned its attention to a small picture

201
00:16:54,661 --> 00:16:58,420
that would indirectly have a big impact on stereo.

202
00:16:59,120 --> 00:17:02,040
Television presented a challenge to the film industry,

203
00:17:02,520 --> 00:17:07,840
and the movies fought back with a new and improved widescreen format, Cinemascope.

204
00:17:07,841 --> 00:17:14,160
We had this wide picture and there was an emotional feeling that people needed stereo sound to go with it.

205
00:17:14,800 --> 00:17:20,020
You know, if the hero was over here on the left and talking, you didn't want his voice coming out of the center,

206
00:17:20,160 --> 00:17:21,540
you wanted his voice coming out of the left.

207
00:17:22,020 --> 00:17:25,940
And that really forced the industry towards stereo in the early 50s.

208
00:17:26,840 --> 00:17:29,840
Stereo was beginning to make its public debut in theaters.

209
00:17:30,620 --> 00:17:36,160
And a radio pioneer, Edwin Armstrong, wanted to bring it to an even larger audience.

210
00:17:38,509 --> 00:17:48,675
Major Armstrong, who actually developed the original circuitry, then made possible AM radio, the idea of the heterodyne circuit, championed FM radio by himself with

211
00:17:48,676 --> 00:17:58,780
some great effort and cost, great personal cost, but introduced the form of broadcasting, which was the best music that any of us could hear in the very early 50s.

212
00:17:58,781 --> 00:18:06,600
The final record was pretty good, but direct broadcast of FM music was the thing that caused me to get into the business, actually.

213
00:18:07,840 --> 00:18:16,160
Radio waves carry the audio signal in two basic ways.

214
00:18:16,660 --> 00:18:19,660
By changing the amplitude or the height of the radio wave,

215
00:18:19,661 --> 00:18:22,900
which is called amplitude modulation, or AM.

216
00:18:23,860 --> 00:18:28,380
Or by changing the frequency of the wave, called frequency modulation, or FM.

217
00:18:29,580 --> 00:18:33,980
FM is a wider, more stable wave that carried less noise or interference.

218
00:18:33,981 --> 00:18:39,980
But it was the early 60s before FM radio was broadcast in stereo.

219
00:18:39,981 --> 00:18:43,980
In the late 50s, most recording was still menoral.

220
00:18:43,981 --> 00:18:46,980
But that was about to change.

221
00:18:46,981 --> 00:18:50,980
Stereo was soon to make a big debut.

222
00:18:53,980 --> 00:18:59,980
By the 1950s, classic crooners from the 30s and 40s like Bing Crosby

223
00:18:59,981 --> 00:19:04,980
and Crosby were being replaced by the gyrating rhythms of rock and roll.

224
00:19:17,980 --> 00:19:22,980
Music was changing and rebellion was embraced by a restless generation.

225
00:19:23,980 --> 00:19:28,980
The power of music was becoming clear, even if most songs were still menorum.

226
00:19:29,980 --> 00:19:33,380
But even if consumers couldn't get their hands on stereo yet,

227
00:19:34,040 --> 00:19:37,160
recording professionals clearly saw the potential.

228
00:19:41,360 --> 00:19:46,680
Well, stereo, of course, opened up the world almost for music from many points of view.

229
00:19:46,681 --> 00:19:53,980
First, you could hear depth and width and height of the sound picture,

230
00:19:54,100 --> 00:19:55,560
the image in front of your eyes.

231
00:19:56,160 --> 00:19:58,880
And you heard much more realistically.

232
00:19:58,881 --> 00:20:04,600
You heard much more of the acoustic space where the recording was being made.

233
00:20:05,900 --> 00:20:12,260
You could close your eyes and place on the stage in front of you the orchestra as it was playing.

234
00:20:12,900 --> 00:20:19,800
Professional recording artists quickly learned that stereo recording presented artistic and technical challenges.

235
00:20:20,720 --> 00:20:22,760
It has to be understood that stereo is an art.

236
00:20:22,761 --> 00:20:31,380
It's an art of placing the microphones where they pick up the orchestra, say, in basic relationships that make sense.

237
00:20:31,740 --> 00:20:35,480
Because if we were to say we really wanted to reproduce what's there,

238
00:20:35,840 --> 00:20:40,220
we'd have to take each instrument of the orchestra and mic it at many angles,

239
00:20:40,740 --> 00:20:43,640
and then we'd have to do that for every instrument of the orchestra,

240
00:20:43,940 --> 00:20:47,100
and we'd wind up with a completely impractical system,

241
00:20:47,420 --> 00:20:51,160
but one that would, in fact, reproduce the orchestra spatially.

242
00:20:51,161 --> 00:20:54,640
A consumer market didn't even exist yet.

243
00:20:55,180 --> 00:21:01,040
But that didn't stop enterprising record producers from testing the fundamentals of stereo recording.

244
00:21:01,960 --> 00:21:06,440
In stereo, you use, at a minimum, two microphones.

245
00:21:07,560 --> 00:21:11,740
Both pointed at the same group of instruments, or same performer.

246
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,240
One microphone for the left channel will pick up,

247
00:21:14,980 --> 00:21:18,900
namely, most loudly, and in some cases a little earlier in time,

248
00:21:18,901 --> 00:21:21,680
the sound from the left half of the orchestra,

249
00:21:21,920 --> 00:21:23,540
but it will still pick up sound from the right.

250
00:21:23,980 --> 00:21:27,540
Each will also pick up some of the sound being reflected

251
00:21:27,541 --> 00:21:30,660
off the walls of the studio or concert hall

252
00:21:30,661 --> 00:21:31,640
where you make the recording.

253
00:21:32,340 --> 00:21:35,280
And that's very important because that is what gives you

254
00:21:35,281 --> 00:21:38,140
the feeling of space surrounding the orchestra.

255
00:21:39,100 --> 00:21:43,380
By 1957, the British company EMI was certain

256
00:21:43,381 --> 00:21:46,240
that stereo would be a big hit with the consumer.

257
00:21:47,320 --> 00:21:48,780
They turned to Keller's invention,

258
00:21:48,900 --> 00:21:51,620
the 45-45 cutting method.

259
00:21:52,360 --> 00:21:57,520
Like Keller's machine, EMIs cut stereo channels on both sides of the same groove.

260
00:21:58,140 --> 00:22:02,640
The master record disc was then used to make other copies of the long playing record.

261
00:22:03,620 --> 00:22:08,220
During playback, one stylist could pick up the two separate channels simultaneously,

262
00:22:09,040 --> 00:22:10,460
creating the stereo effect.

263
00:22:11,020 --> 00:22:15,400
One characteristic of the record business is that it evolves very, very quickly.

264
00:22:15,401 --> 00:22:21,020
It starts with an idea and a desire to improve and record in a better way

265
00:22:21,021 --> 00:22:26,920
or to give more opportunities for different ways to use the technology.

266
00:22:27,340 --> 00:22:30,140
Then there are the people who begin to use it,

267
00:22:30,620 --> 00:22:35,680
and then there's always the problem of how to get it into the home of the consumer.

268
00:22:36,220 --> 00:22:40,220
It wasn't long before American companies, not wanting to be left out,

269
00:22:40,640 --> 00:22:43,920
developed their own 45-45 cutting machine.

270
00:22:45,400 --> 00:22:51,260
When it was put on display in New York, one young enthusiast couldn't wait.

271
00:22:52,800 --> 00:22:57,040
He apparently took this Westrex cutter head that could cut at a 45 degree angle,

272
00:22:57,360 --> 00:23:02,120
out of this hi-fi show, went to a lathe that was normally cutting monophonically,

273
00:23:02,320 --> 00:23:05,100
and cut a record and released the world's first stereophonic record.

274
00:23:05,620 --> 00:23:09,420
That changed everything because all of a sudden you could have stereophonic sound.

275
00:23:10,100 --> 00:23:14,280
Stereo had arrived, but the audio industry wasn't prepared.

276
00:23:14,281 --> 00:23:19,280
Hi-fi sets were big, bulky, and designed for mono playback.

277
00:23:19,281 --> 00:23:25,280
New equipment was needed, and the loudspeaker was the first place audio engineers looked.

278
00:23:25,281 --> 00:23:32,280
To generate bass frequencies, the lowest register frequencies, you have to be able to move a lot of air.

279
00:23:32,281 --> 00:23:43,280
This means a speaker cone, which can go forward and backward, have an excursion that's relatively large.

280
00:23:43,281 --> 00:23:48,440
because it just has the very lowest frequencies it pumps, literally pumps.

281
00:23:49,020 --> 00:23:53,660
You needed a large speaker box, and with stereo, you needed two of them.

282
00:23:55,940 --> 00:24:01,480
Ed Veltcher, with the help of Henry Close, used the current research to design a new speaker.

283
00:24:02,120 --> 00:24:04,640
He called it the acoustic suspension speaker.

284
00:24:05,260 --> 00:24:07,980
It was a small box with a lot of sound.

285
00:24:08,120 --> 00:24:12,380
In the early days, you had gigantic loudspeakers that were extremely efficient.

286
00:24:12,381 --> 00:24:16,680
It was a big box, and the excursion of the driver was minuscule,

287
00:24:17,120 --> 00:24:20,880
and it relied upon other aspects of sound reproduction to get the big sound.

288
00:24:21,480 --> 00:24:22,980
Very efficient in speaker.

289
00:24:23,720 --> 00:24:25,960
The acoustic suspension speaker did the opposite.

290
00:24:26,160 --> 00:24:29,560
It required a lot of mechanical motion to get sound.

291
00:24:29,840 --> 00:24:31,960
Very inefficient, required a lot of power.

292
00:24:32,580 --> 00:24:37,840
By the early 60s, Veltcher and Close were ready to try their luck with the stereo stores.

293
00:24:37,841 --> 00:24:46,360
you carried a speaker into the store and the maven there that pronounced their view of the

294
00:24:46,361 --> 00:24:50,360
speaker and like the fact that you could carry in through the front door or something which

295
00:24:50,361 --> 00:24:55,080
sounded like that was so much different than anything before that it uh it was lots of fun

296
00:24:55,081 --> 00:25:00,180
to be there and see that happen and it did more important change the way people thought about and

297
00:25:00,181 --> 00:25:07,260
made speakers thereafter they called their speaker the ar1 and after many tests they were certain

298
00:25:07,261 --> 00:25:09,980
that they had come close to audio perfection.

299
00:25:11,120 --> 00:25:14,460
The acoustic suspension woofer changed everything

300
00:25:14,461 --> 00:25:18,180
because all of a sudden you could have a box, a small box.

301
00:25:31,380 --> 00:25:34,980
To walk into someone's house and all of a sudden hear

302
00:25:34,981 --> 00:25:39,960
full-range sound with extended highs where cymbals sounded like cymbals and

303
00:25:39,961 --> 00:25:43,960
you heard a guy playing a bass and it was deep bass

304
00:25:53,040 --> 00:25:56,860
I never forget the first time I heard that I you know I was a little kid but I

305
00:25:56,861 --> 00:26:00,960
heard it it was like I wanted to jump into that box and and take it home with

306
00:26:00,961 --> 00:26:07,960
me and you know and have that.

307
00:26:07,961 --> 00:26:13,340
At that time you had a community with stores, with musically knowledgeable people, with

308
00:26:13,341 --> 00:26:14,368
a few magazines.

309
00:26:14,369 --> 00:26:18,800
You had a world that really needed a lot of new things and it was one

310
00:26:18,801 --> 00:26:19,800
of them.

311
00:26:19,801 --> 00:26:27,880
Music was becoming a big part of the way Americans relaxed and had fun.

312
00:26:27,881 --> 00:26:30,180
High-end audio in the 50s was a lifestyle thing.

313
00:26:30,380 --> 00:26:31,360
It was a bachelor pad.

314
00:26:31,460 --> 00:26:32,740
It was along with your martini shaker.

315
00:26:33,140 --> 00:26:36,520
It was, you know, something you'd have and, you know, come up and hear my stereo rig.

316
00:26:36,780 --> 00:26:37,940
And then there were test records.

317
00:26:38,300 --> 00:26:39,560
The gimmick was, you know, ping-pong.

318
00:26:39,640 --> 00:26:43,740
You'd have a guy playing ping-pong and you'd sit there and you'd hear the ping-pong ball going back and forth.

319
00:26:43,741 --> 00:26:48,460
Or they'd have a person walking around the room and they'd say, now I'm on the left, now I'm in the center, now I'm on the right.

320
00:26:49,160 --> 00:26:52,220
Stereo was fun, but there was more to it.

321
00:26:52,760 --> 00:26:56,720
Stereo music had the power to shape the mind and touch the heart.

322
00:26:56,721 --> 00:27:09,104
Music is very emotional and a good recording involves the listener and that becomes a part of your memory and is associated with different events and different times

323
00:27:09,105 --> 00:27:10,080
of your life.

324
00:27:10,480 --> 00:27:17,020
And that's why you cherish music, which you learned many, many years ago throughout your life.

325
00:27:17,540 --> 00:27:25,800
And that's what makes very loyal fans of different artists and different recordings and so forth.

326
00:27:25,801 --> 00:27:30,580
By the early 60s, the stereo revolution was in full swing.

327
00:27:31,380 --> 00:27:32,720
Music was accessible.

328
00:27:33,560 --> 00:27:34,420
It was affordable.

329
00:27:35,160 --> 00:27:36,740
And it was in stereo.

330
00:27:37,500 --> 00:27:40,860
New technologies were opening up new paths for stereo,

331
00:27:40,861 --> 00:27:47,400
and one of them would put the power of a large stereo system literally into the palm of the consumer's hand.

332
00:27:55,800 --> 00:28:00,720
The turbulent 60s brought catharsis and change to the American landscape,

333
00:28:01,080 --> 00:28:03,660
and at the center of the revolution was the music.

334
00:28:04,440 --> 00:28:07,960
Americans wanted their music loud, clear, and in stereo.

335
00:28:08,240 --> 00:28:11,940
A new generation was turning on and tuning in,

336
00:28:12,260 --> 00:28:14,840
and they were doing it with stereophonic sound.

337
00:28:15,640 --> 00:28:19,760
If you go back to the 60s, what you see happening is you see audio or stereo

338
00:28:19,761 --> 00:28:21,720
becoming a mass-market phenomenon,

339
00:28:21,721 --> 00:28:27,160
moving from a hobbyist mode in the late 50s, early 60s

340
00:28:27,161 --> 00:28:29,820
to something that everyone could use and appreciate.

341
00:28:30,760 --> 00:28:34,160
And, you know, by the time the late 60s rolled around,

342
00:28:34,360 --> 00:28:37,700
stereo was the word for a complete system.

343
00:28:38,380 --> 00:28:41,120
The AR-1 had revolutionized speakers

344
00:28:41,121 --> 00:28:44,260
by putting a lot of power into a small box.

345
00:28:45,080 --> 00:28:48,400
The transistor was about to do the same for the amplifier.

346
00:28:48,401 --> 00:28:54,460
A transistor is basically a switch, but if it's configured the right way, it can actually

347
00:28:54,461 --> 00:28:57,160
amplify the sound.

348
00:28:57,161 --> 00:29:01,560
The transistor, like the vacuum tube, worked like a valve.

349
00:29:01,561 --> 00:29:06,880
The audio signal was fed into the vacuum tube or the transistor, which would then tell the

350
00:29:06,881 --> 00:29:11,160
power source in the amplifier how much power to send to the speaker.

351
00:29:11,161 --> 00:29:17,880
The transistor, a fraction of the size of the vacuum tube, was just as effective.

352
00:29:17,881 --> 00:29:20,280
Before the transistor, you had to use vacuum tubes.

353
00:29:20,281 --> 00:29:24,000
Vacuum tubes were big, they were cumbersome, they had a lot of heat.

354
00:29:24,001 --> 00:29:27,480
The heat would create problems with the other components in the box.

355
00:29:27,481 --> 00:29:31,600
You'd need output transformers so the things were very heavy, very complicated.

356
00:29:31,601 --> 00:29:36,580
Once you had solid state, once you had a transistor involved in it, the heat was gone, the transformer

357
00:29:36,581 --> 00:29:41,380
was gone, you could put these things together very inexpensively in a very small package

358
00:29:41,381 --> 00:29:46,720
and you'd have portability and reliability and that made audio available to everybody.

359
00:29:47,880 --> 00:29:54,200
The transistor established a clear trend, smaller systems, but bigger sound.

360
00:29:56,280 --> 00:30:04,240
Companies like KLH, founded by Henry Close, were using a little ingenuity to create a great big sound.

361
00:30:05,780 --> 00:30:10,020
I had learned of a way to make luggage, structural pieces.

362
00:30:11,520 --> 00:30:15,200
Garrard, company in England, finally made a really good changer.

363
00:30:15,201 --> 00:30:18,380
These things came together in what was then called Model 11.

364
00:30:18,560 --> 00:30:22,960
It was the first use of power transistors for a consumer product that I know of.

365
00:30:23,500 --> 00:30:27,740
Small speakers, small power amplifier, all put in a case you could carry around.

366
00:30:28,320 --> 00:30:32,560
What KLH did in the 1960s was to popularize the stereophonic concept.

367
00:30:33,000 --> 00:30:36,440
They made the suitcase stereo that practically everybody took to college with them

368
00:30:36,441 --> 00:30:40,460
and made stereo into a wide-scale consumer phenomenon

369
00:30:40,461 --> 00:30:43,820
from the hobbyist phenomenon it had been at an earlier time.

370
00:30:43,821 --> 00:30:46,300
Americans love the convenience.

371
00:30:47,260 --> 00:30:51,100
They wanted music tailored to their own lifestyles and interests.

372
00:30:51,760 --> 00:30:54,960
Home recording had long been the domain of the ardent hobbyist,

373
00:30:55,360 --> 00:30:58,460
but Ray Dolby was about to change that.

374
00:30:59,440 --> 00:31:01,800
Dolby had grown up in Northern California.

375
00:31:02,300 --> 00:31:04,360
A bright student and ardent music lover,

376
00:31:04,480 --> 00:31:07,580
he worked as an engineer at Ampex in the 1950s.

377
00:31:08,540 --> 00:31:12,260
Dolby became fascinated with the problem of tape noise.

378
00:31:12,261 --> 00:31:19,260
The noise or hiss on a recorded tape was a function of the natural properties of sound.

379
00:31:19,261 --> 00:31:22,260
Sound waves differ in two basic parameters.

380
00:31:22,261 --> 00:31:28,260
They differ in frequency, which is how many times a second the sound is vibrating.

381
00:31:28,261 --> 00:31:31,260
The more times it vibrates, the higher the pitch.

382
00:31:31,261 --> 00:31:36,260
And they vary in intensity or amplitude.

383
00:31:36,261 --> 00:31:45,680
A very weak sound vibration, low amplitude or low intensity, we generally say amplitude, is a very soft sound.

384
00:31:46,760 --> 00:31:50,120
A very high amplitude sound is a very loud one.

385
00:31:51,460 --> 00:31:58,640
In fact, an audio signal, like the sound of my voice, is very complex because at any given moment,

386
00:31:59,520 --> 00:32:04,200
you're hearing a bunch of different frequencies at different amplitudes.

387
00:32:04,201 --> 00:32:09,200
By 1967, Dolby had solved the problem of tape noise.

388
00:32:09,201 --> 00:32:19,200
Dolby noise reduction, as he called it, relied on a complex circuit that essentially separated out the frequencies and manipulated the signal,

389
00:32:19,201 --> 00:32:23,200
so that in the end, the noise was eliminated from the tape.

390
00:32:24,200 --> 00:32:30,200
The simplest way to think about how Dolby noise reduction works is to think of it as a kind of compressor.

391
00:32:30,201 --> 00:32:35,960
but unlike conventional compressors which lower the loud signal, this is the kind of broadcast

392
00:32:35,961 --> 00:32:40,520
compressor we're used to, what happens with Dolby noise reduction is that before the signal is

393
00:32:40,521 --> 00:32:45,080
recorded onto the tape the low-level signals are brought up instead of the high-level signals

394
00:32:45,081 --> 00:32:46,279
being brought down.

395
00:32:46,280 --> 00:32:51,140
Signal is recorded that way onto the tape then in playback the decoder leaves

396
00:32:51,141 --> 00:32:55,880
the high-level signals untouched again but takes down the low-level signals restoring the original

397
00:32:55,881 --> 00:32:59,880
dynamics. But of course the playback unit can't tell the difference between the

398
00:32:59,881 --> 00:33:03,920
low-level signals and the tape hiss, so the hiss goes down along with the low-level

399
00:33:03,921 --> 00:33:08,480
signals. The original signal is completely restored.

400
00:33:09,380 --> 00:33:15,380
The first system called Dolby A was expensive and used primarily for

401
00:33:15,381 --> 00:33:21,480
professional recordings. Many good recordings were made in the late 1960s

402
00:33:21,481 --> 00:33:23,180
with Dolby A noise reduction.

403
00:33:23,740 --> 00:33:25,940
And they drew the attention of Henry Close,

404
00:33:26,400 --> 00:33:31,340
who, upon hearing this, got on an airplane to London the next day

405
00:33:31,341 --> 00:33:35,800
and went to see Ray Dolby and tried to talk him into a consumer system.

406
00:33:36,400 --> 00:33:40,140
When Phillips introduced the cassette in 1963,

407
00:33:40,920 --> 00:33:45,260
many dismissed it as a low-quality, inexpensive recording medium.

408
00:33:46,200 --> 00:33:50,520
Henry Close thought that all it needed was a little help from Ray Dolby's system.

409
00:33:50,521 --> 00:33:59,420
Henry Close saw that this small new medium that Phillips had introduced as a dictation medium called the cassette,

410
00:34:00,040 --> 00:34:10,040
when combined with chromium dioxide tape made by DuPont and companding noise reduction, Dolby B, could be a viable music format.

411
00:34:10,041 --> 00:34:17,060
He pushed the limits of the envelope of a newly introduced technology for one purpose into a whole different purpose

412
00:34:17,061 --> 00:34:21,900
and came up with a medium that is one of the most ubiquitous on earth.

413
00:34:22,220 --> 00:34:27,480
For the first time, the consumer could not only play but record their favorite music.

414
00:34:28,500 --> 00:34:31,100
The quality of stereo equipment was improving.

415
00:34:31,900 --> 00:34:35,420
The consumer was expecting better and better stereo products.

416
00:34:41,000 --> 00:34:45,240
By the 1970s, stereo sound on films was possible,

417
00:34:45,241 --> 00:34:47,640
but it was no longer practical.

418
00:34:48,840 --> 00:34:52,120
There was a stereo process out there in the film industry

419
00:34:52,121 --> 00:34:55,620
which is four-track magnetic, which is used on 35mm prints.

420
00:34:56,500 --> 00:34:59,420
And in this process, in addition to the picture,

421
00:34:59,880 --> 00:35:03,060
there are four mag tracks striped across the film.

422
00:35:03,500 --> 00:35:07,000
The good theatres would have six or four tracks of magnetic tape

423
00:35:07,001 --> 00:35:09,540
that would have to be attached to the sides of the film

424
00:35:09,541 --> 00:35:12,600
and then run through a recorder one at a time.

425
00:35:12,840 --> 00:35:13,560
It was fragile.

426
00:35:13,561 --> 00:35:16,180
If the film broke and you spliced it together, you'd have all kinds of blips.

427
00:35:16,260 --> 00:35:17,120
It was very problematic.

428
00:35:18,100 --> 00:35:24,640
Ray Dolby and Yoann Allen were certain that Dolby noise reduction could be very helpful to Hollywood.

429
00:35:25,700 --> 00:35:28,520
I started visiting film studios saying,

430
00:35:29,160 --> 00:35:31,080
good afternoon, would you like to buy my noise reduction?

431
00:35:31,840 --> 00:35:36,660
And was more often than not greeted by blank looks and stares and no thank you very much.

432
00:35:37,420 --> 00:35:42,520
And in fact, our experiments adding noise reduction revealed no great improvement.

433
00:35:42,521 --> 00:35:47,580
and I started examining why, and this led to a lengthy program

434
00:35:47,581 --> 00:35:50,180
of looking at the whole film sound chain.

435
00:35:50,680 --> 00:35:55,920
What they found was using Dolby noise reduction solved only part of the problem.

436
00:35:57,040 --> 00:36:02,200
A way would have to be found to record with four channels on a narrow optical soundtrack.

437
00:36:04,100 --> 00:36:09,140
Dolby Labs used a technique that had been left over from the Quadrophonic Era

438
00:36:09,141 --> 00:36:15,320
to stuff four channels worth of information into two tracks available on the film's soundtrack.

439
00:36:16,200 --> 00:36:20,700
By using this quadraphonic technique but turning it around in a whole new way,

440
00:36:21,400 --> 00:36:27,740
they reinvent multi-channel surround sound with three front channels, left, center, and right,

441
00:36:28,160 --> 00:36:30,680
and a monaural surround sound channel.

442
00:36:31,520 --> 00:36:35,080
Dolby's system dramatically improved theater sound.

443
00:36:36,040 --> 00:36:38,680
We were moderately successful with A Star is Born in the theaters,

444
00:36:38,681 --> 00:36:42,660
It was apparent that the Dolby stereo was going to catch on.

445
00:36:43,520 --> 00:36:46,200
And we were fortunate to meet with Gary Kurtz and George Lucas,

446
00:36:46,800 --> 00:36:49,880
who determined that Star Wars should be released with a Dolby stereo format,

447
00:36:50,460 --> 00:36:53,360
which, of course, did us a great deal of good in the theaters,

448
00:36:53,620 --> 00:36:55,900
because Star Wars was probably the first exposure

449
00:36:55,901 --> 00:37:00,000
that 99% of their audiences had ever had to stereo in a theater.

450
00:37:01,060 --> 00:37:05,980
The versatile Dolby system could be played either on existing or new projectors,

451
00:37:05,981 --> 00:37:09,320
making it a big hit with the audience and the exhibitors.

452
00:37:10,440 --> 00:37:13,100
Dolby and multiplexes were bringing audiences back.

453
00:37:13,580 --> 00:37:17,680
Then came a revolutionary designed by Tom Holman for Lucasfilms

454
00:37:17,681 --> 00:37:19,480
called Tom Holman Crossover.

455
00:37:19,900 --> 00:37:21,760
It became known as THX.

456
00:37:22,580 --> 00:37:26,600
What I realized was something of all places from rock and roll roadshows.

457
00:37:27,100 --> 00:37:32,440
What rock and roll roadshows show is that as you move from venue to venue,

458
00:37:32,441 --> 00:37:35,840
the sound is different and always affected by the venue.

459
00:37:35,980 --> 00:37:42,600
So the only way to get the movie to sound the same in different venues was to have control over the acoustics.

460
00:37:43,040 --> 00:37:49,040
By taking into account every acoustical factor, such as reverberation and echoes in the theater,

461
00:37:49,700 --> 00:37:58,660
THX provided each movie house with identical uniform acoustics and audiences the optimum audio experience possible.

462
00:37:59,720 --> 00:38:05,080
Just as film had led to innovation in the 40s after the release of Disney's Fantasia,

463
00:38:05,081 --> 00:38:09,760
film stereo was about to revolutionize home stereo in the 80s.

464
00:38:10,500 --> 00:38:13,620
The digital revolution was just around the corner.

465
00:38:21,220 --> 00:38:26,080
By the late 1970s, the world was on the brink of the digital revolution.

466
00:38:26,540 --> 00:38:28,420
But first came miniaturization.

467
00:38:29,100 --> 00:38:32,660
Stereo had been available in the home and in the car for over 20 years.

468
00:38:32,661 --> 00:38:36,800
the ultimate in sound personalization was about to arrive.

469
00:38:37,940 --> 00:38:40,320
When Sony introduced the Walkman in 1979,

470
00:38:41,040 --> 00:38:46,160
that was a remarkable change in the way we were able to listen to music.

471
00:38:47,020 --> 00:38:51,900
We went from portable carry-along radios

472
00:38:51,901 --> 00:38:55,200
to this small handheld device

473
00:38:55,201 --> 00:38:58,840
that provided really excellent full-bodied stereo sound

474
00:38:58,841 --> 00:39:01,320
through a really lightweight collapsible pair of headphones.

475
00:39:01,321 --> 00:39:09,600
that was a revolution that caught on very quickly because suddenly you could take sound with you wherever you wanted to go.

476
00:39:10,100 --> 00:39:15,560
Not only was the size of the system changing, the format was about to change as well.

477
00:39:16,600 --> 00:39:22,860
The computer was transforming life in America, and stereo was caught up in the digital revolution.

478
00:39:22,861 --> 00:39:30,760
When in 1983 Sony introduced the compact disc, music distribution was changed forever.

479
00:39:31,320 --> 00:39:35,920
As the CD revolutionized the way people listened at home,

480
00:39:36,660 --> 00:39:40,620
motion picture sound engineers turned to technology from the past

481
00:39:40,621 --> 00:39:45,300
to introduce audiences to the technology of the future.

482
00:39:46,200 --> 00:39:49,640
The Disney studio was some 60 years ahead of its time

483
00:39:49,641 --> 00:39:52,600
in the artistic development of surround sound

484
00:39:52,601 --> 00:39:56,500
because they knew what an engaging experience this could be.

485
00:39:57,040 --> 00:39:59,840
After all, in this way, the film sound experience

486
00:39:59,841 --> 00:40:01,380
was made to match perception.

487
00:40:02,000 --> 00:40:03,580
The picture was in front of us

488
00:40:03,581 --> 00:40:05,780
because our vision is front-dominated.

489
00:40:06,220 --> 00:40:07,780
The sound was all around us

490
00:40:07,781 --> 00:40:10,120
because we can hear sound from all around us.

491
00:40:10,400 --> 00:40:14,520
It was a fulfillment of the capability of human perception.

492
00:40:15,780 --> 00:40:18,760
The work begun by THX in speaker placement

493
00:40:18,761 --> 00:40:20,820
was continued by Surround Sound.

494
00:40:22,120 --> 00:40:24,320
Audiences loved Surround Sound,

495
00:40:24,440 --> 00:40:27,660
and soon they wanted the technology available in their homes.

496
00:40:27,661 --> 00:40:36,040
Over the course of more than 60 years now, what has happened is the motion picture theater came out with a sound system.

497
00:40:36,460 --> 00:40:39,420
Then the home came out with something to compete with it.

498
00:40:39,840 --> 00:40:44,760
Then the motion picture theater came back with another and better sound system.

499
00:40:45,220 --> 00:40:52,080
We play something, a game I call technological leapfrog, where in each era we see an improvement in one

500
00:40:52,081 --> 00:40:56,060
and then a corresponding improvement in the other one to remain competitive.

501
00:40:56,061 --> 00:41:05,020
The high quality stereo that people expected at theaters was about to leapfrog into our living rooms.

502
00:41:05,740 --> 00:41:08,940
For most people, surround sound is subliminal when it's done right.

503
00:41:09,740 --> 00:41:14,500
Certain sounds occur on the front stereo stage, we call it.

504
00:41:14,720 --> 00:41:16,480
They match the action of the movie.

505
00:41:17,120 --> 00:41:25,100
But surround sound, like the wind in the trees, puts you there with the actors in the action.

506
00:41:25,101 --> 00:41:30,920
That's what's different about a mono 1928 movie and a 1998 movie.

507
00:41:31,220 --> 00:41:34,900
The sound designer has the opportunity to bring you into the action.

508
00:41:35,600 --> 00:41:40,880
In today's audio labs, sound engineers are working to perfect surround sound,

509
00:41:41,520 --> 00:41:46,360
putting the power and complexity of a theater system into the hands of the consumer.

510
00:41:47,440 --> 00:41:53,120
As the technology proliferates, new equipment and new methods are helping audio engineers explore.

511
00:41:53,121 --> 00:42:00,120
You actually can get to the point with the sound where you might literally be fooled.

512
00:42:00,121 --> 00:42:04,120
And all it has to do is work kind of subliminally.

513
00:42:04,121 --> 00:42:14,891
I mean, if you're absorbed in the movie, if the director or the sound engineer in the movie can give you that subliminal impression that you were in the kind of place

514
00:42:14,892 --> 00:42:21,120
where the action on the screen could be happening, it really, really enhances the realism of it.

515
00:42:21,121 --> 00:42:27,540
And as the technology advances, engineers work to expand the possibilities for sound.

516
00:42:29,800 --> 00:42:35,000
One of the most important ingredients in the history of all sound reproduction is the number of channels.

517
00:42:35,560 --> 00:42:39,220
We've moved from 1 to 2, and now from 2 to 5.

518
00:42:39,660 --> 00:42:43,080
And in the future, we'll go beyond 5 to 10 and more.

519
00:42:43,081 --> 00:42:51,860
And the reason for this is that it's ever more stimulating than a simple box loudspeaker sitting on your desk.

520
00:42:52,020 --> 00:43:03,939
We have widened out horizontally and then vertically the reproduction to get closer and closer to the ideal of being embedded or immersed in a fully stereophonic, that

521
00:43:03,940 --> 00:43:05,580
is, solid, sound field.

522
00:43:05,581 --> 00:43:13,580
In Sound Labs, experiments continue on innovations that dazzle even the most finely tuned ear.

523
00:43:19,580 --> 00:43:24,580
Computers are opening up new doors for personalizing the sound experience.

524
00:43:30,580 --> 00:43:32,580
From graphic games.

525
00:43:35,580 --> 00:43:40,160
to sound modeling.

526
00:43:46,720 --> 00:43:49,620
Stereo is entering a new dimension.

527
00:43:51,100 --> 00:43:54,800
Stereo has grown from humble beginnings in Edison's lab

528
00:43:54,801 --> 00:43:58,020
into a complex computer-driven technology.

529
00:43:58,860 --> 00:44:00,880
But one thing hasn't changed.

530
00:44:05,580 --> 00:44:08,700
Music has the power to transform society.

531
00:44:09,640 --> 00:44:13,580
And stereo has taken that power, given it dimension, scope,

532
00:44:13,940 --> 00:44:16,720
and brought it into all of our lives.

